🛡️ Security Design

The project follows several secure-development principles.

Passwords are not stored as plaintext

Credential data is encrypted before being written to disk.

Master password is not stored

Only the information required for key derivation is stored.

Argon2id is used for key derivation

This makes password guessing more expensive than using a fast general-purpose hash.

AES-256-GCM provides authenticated encryption

This protects confidentiality and detects unauthorized modification.

Random salts are used

Each vault receives a unique salt.

Fresh nonces are generated

Each encryption operation generates a new nonce.

Cryptographically secure randomness is used

Password generation uses Python's secrets module.

Vault permissions are restricted

On Unix-like systems, vault files are created with:

0600

This gives the owner read/write access while denying access to other users.

Atomic saving is used

The application writes to a temporary file before replacing the existing vault, reducing the risk of leaving a partially written vault after a failed save.

🎯 Threat Model

The project primarily addresses the scenario where an attacker obtains the encrypted vault file but does not know the master password.

For example:

Attacker obtains vault.json
          ↓
Credential data is encrypted
          ↓
Master password required
          ↓
Argon2id derives encryption key
          ↓
AES-256-GCM attempts decryption
          ↓
Correct password → Decrypt
Wrong password   → Reject

AES-GCM authentication also allows the application to detect modification of encrypted data.

⚠️ Security Limitations

This is an educational cybersecurity and software-engineering project.

It has not been professionally security audited and should not be considered equivalent to a production password manager.

It cannot protect against every possible threat.

Examples include:

Malware running on the host machine
Keyloggers
A compromised operating system
An attacker controlling the running Python process
Memory extraction
A stolen master password
Compromised Python dependencies
Vulnerabilities in the operating system
Physical compromise of an unlocked machine

The project is intended to demonstrate secure design and practical cryptography rather than replace professionally audited password-management software.

